-# PicoMarQ 1.3.3 — validation and release status
+# PicoMarQ 1.3.4 — validation and release status
 
-**Changes since 1.3.2** (`history/changes-since-1.3.2.patch`):
+**Change since 1.3.3** (`history/changes-since-1.3.3.patch`): a newer engine, and nothing else in the program.
 
-- **Undo can no longer empty a document.** Through 1.3.2, Ctrl+Z in a document that had just been opened, or pressed once more than there were changes, took out the whole text. Ctrl+Y brought it back, but a save in between wrote an empty file.
-  - *Why.* Putting a document's text into its editor was an ordinary change for the editor (TipTap's `setContent`), and so the first step of the undo history.
-  - *How.* The load is marked as not belonging to the history (`addToHistory: false`, `src/components/editor/Editor.tsx`). Revert to Saved starts a new editor and loads the same way. The return from the Markdown source view is still a step of its own, as before: one Undo there changes nothing that can be seen, the next ones take back earlier typing, and they stop at the opened text.
-- **Red lines in long paragraphs.** Chromium rechecks spelling by itself only around the caret: a whole paragraph of up to 1,024 characters, otherwise 512 characters on either side. Through 1.3.2, a paragraph longer than that (about 170 words) lost the red lines farther from the caret whenever the editor changed text itself, and they stayed away. Two causes, two cures:
-  - *Text replaced as a whole* (`src/lib/minimal-text-updates.ts`). When the editor changed a piece of text it assigned the whole new string, and Chromium then drops every spelling mark in that piece. Smart Typography does this for each curly apostrophe, quotation mark, dash and ellipsis, and Undo of typing does it too. Now only the characters that differ are replaced; Chromium keeps the marks before them and moves the ones after them along. Typing with Smart Typography and Undo of typing cost no red line anymore.
-  - *Text built anew* (`src/components/editor/QuietRecheck.ts`, `recheckSpellingQuietly` in `src/lib/spelling.ts`). Bold, Italic, Enter, joining paragraphs, Undo of those, and Edit > Apply Smart Typography put part of a paragraph into new pieces of text, which have no marks. After such a change the editor asks Chromium for a check of the whole document of a kind that does not blink. Chromium makes such a check by itself when the length of the text has changed by more than 1,024 characters since its last one, and it then replaces the marks piece by piece instead of clearing them first. The editor changes the length Chromium counts without touching the document: an invisible filler at the end of the editor element is made 1,100 characters longer or shorter. In the test paragraph (1,672 characters, a misspelling 800 characters before the caret and one 820 after it) the red line after a word made bold or italic was back 1.39 to 1.45 seconds later (8 tries), and no other red line went away. The same now holds in short paragraphs for the cases listed for 1.3.2 (Undo of a deleted selection, Bold on and off, Italic).
-  - *The two kinds of recheck together.* The rechecks of 1.2.7 to 1.3.2 (after a paste, Replace, Ignore All, Clear Custom Dictionary and a return from the source view) clear every red line and bring them back about a second later; they are unchanged. Such a recheck goes over the document one second after it is asked for. A change made before that is covered by it, so no quiet check is asked for then; a change made after it gets its quiet check. A first version skipped the quiet check for a second and a half: Bold between 1.0 and 1.2 seconds after a blinking recheck then lost the red line after the word for good (8 times of 9). With the rule as it is now, the line came back 30 times of 30, for Bold at ten moments between 0.3 and 2.5 seconds after the recheck.
-  - *The filler* is an element at the end of the document, of no size, that cannot be selected, is not read aloud, and is not part of the text, of a copy or of the saved file. Two faults of a trial build, found while measuring speed, were corrected before this release. An empty filler was a place of its own for Chromium's caret: Ctrl+End put the caret behind it and not at the end of the last line, and Chromium does not scroll to a caret there. The editor put the caret right a moment later but scrolls only when that moment is shorter than a twentieth of a second, so in a 2 MB document that had just been opened Ctrl+End left the window where it was. The filler is now never empty, and Ctrl+End, Ctrl+Shift+End and the arrow keys at the end of a document do what they do in 1.3.2, the first movement of the caret included. And the last block of a document was no longer the last child of the editor element, from which the stylesheet takes the bottom margin, so every document was 13 pixels taller; one rule in `src/App.css` gives the block before the filler the same treatment. Pictures of the window with eighteen documents, each ending in a different kind of block (and one of them scrolled to its end), are the same in 1.3.2 and 1.3.3 to the pixel; against the trial build the same comparison showed the 13 pixels in every one.
-  - *Tried and taken out.* Drawing frames after every caret movement, so that Chromium's own check gets its turn in a window that stands still (see Limitations). Without it the red lines were back three seconds after the caret returned in 85 of 86 tries, with it in 16 of 16; those numbers show no difference, so it is not in this version.
-- **Find understands Smart Typography** (`src/lib/find-text.ts`). A straight apostrophe or quotation mark typed in the Find box also finds the curly ones, exactly two hyphens also find an em dash, and three periods also find an ellipsis: the conversions of Smart Typography read backward. It works one way only. A curly quote, an em dash or an ellipsis in the Find box finds itself and nothing else, so an exact search stays possible, and an em dash never matches the hyphens of a table line or of code.
-- **Find finds a phrase across formatting** (`src/lib/find-in-document.ts`). In the formatted view a paragraph is now searched as one text. Through 1.3.2 each stretch of the same formatting was searched by itself: with `the *Odyssey* in` in the text, "the Odyssey" was not found.
-- **Replace follows Smart Typography.** When Smart Typography is on, the text of the Replace box is converted as if it had been typed at that place: curly quotes that open or close by what stands before them, an em dash, an ellipsis. Inside code, and in the source view wherever Smart Typography does not act on typing (code, math, front matter, link addresses, HTML), it goes in as typed; with Smart Typography off, everywhere.
-- **Replace All replaces every match.** Through 1.3.2 it replaced at most 500 at a time. Find still lists, highlights and steps through at most 500 matches and now shows "1/500+" when there are more. Replace All changes each paragraph once, which keeps very many matches from freezing the window (see Speed), and one Undo takes all of it back.
-- **Pictures in shared folders on other computers are not fetched** (`src/lib/desktop.ts`, `desktop/shared-folders.cjs`, `desktop/main.cjs`).
-  - *Why.* On Windows, asking another computer for a file makes Windows sign in to it, which gives that computer the user's name and a form of the password that can be attacked. A document from someone else could point a picture at a computer set up for that (`![](//server/share/x.png)`, or the same written as a `file:` address). Opening the document was enough.
-  - *How.* The page gives such a picture no address, however the address is written (two slashes, `file://server/…`, `file:////server/…`, backslashes, percent signs). And the main process stops every request for a file on another computer before it goes out, whatever in the page asked for it. The computer the document itself is on is the exception, and so is the one PicoMarQ runs from: a document in a shared folder shows the pictures from that computer. A mapped drive letter counts as this computer. The document is not changed; every address stays in its text.
-  - *What else was looked at.* Nothing else in a document asked another computer for anything: markup in a diagram's labels is shown as text, the math commands that fetch are off, other HTML (video, frames, style sheets, objects, styles) is dropped, and a link does nothing until it is clicked and is then opened only as a web or mail link.
-- Version 1.3.3 in `package.json`, the documents, the text of the web page, and the executable's properties.
+- **Electron 44.11.5 in place of 44.6.0.** PicoMarQ runs on Electron, which carries a copy of the Chromium browser engine and of Node.js. The new release has Chromium 152.0.7977.169 (was 152.0.7977.130) and Node.js 24.22.3 (was 24.21.0). It is the same generation of Chromium with the security and bug fixes of the seventeen Electron releases since, so the page engine that the spelling features lean on is the one they were built for. Of the release notes in between, those of 44.7.0, 44.8.0, 44.9.0, 44.10.0, 44.11.0 and 44.11.5 were read: fixes, and nothing that changes a function PicoMarQ uses. Some of the fixes are to the request filter that 1.3.3's barrier against pictures on other computers is built on (two crashes, and requests made by web workers now pass through it too; PicoMarQ starts no web workers).
+- **The program is that of 1.3.3.** The compiled program in `dist/` and the main-process files in `desktop/` are byte for byte the same. In `package.json` and `package-lock.json` only the version and the entry for Electron change; no other part was moved (the lock file differs from 1.3.3's in those two entries only).
+- **The packaging script** (`scripts/package-windows.cjs`) now compares the checksum of Electron's Windows package with the published one itself, can use a copy fetched beforehand (`PICOMARQ_ELECTRON_ZIP`), and writes `RELEASE-VALIDATION.txt`. It is used again because the engine is new: every release from 1.2.4 to 1.3.3 kept the runtime files of the release before it.
+- **One test was corrected** (`tests/desktop-smoke.cjs`). Before it presses Ctrl+W in one of several windows, it now waits until every window is on the screen and the window it means has kept the focus. The reason is under “Tests” and “Found while testing” below. The program was not changed for it.
+- Version 1.3.4 in `package.json`, the documents, the text of the web page, and the executable's properties; the line for Electron in `THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt` (its license text is unchanged).
 
